 and Japan to Greece and Rome to the Americas and Oceania, but the sport we=
 know today has its origins in a meeting at the Freemasons=E2=80=99 Tavern =
in Covent Garden, London, on 26 October 1863. By the 19th century there wer=
e countless variations of the game being played across Britain. For those f=
rom the public schools, this was a matter of intense frustration. They woul=
d get to university, meet footballers from elsewhere and then find that bef=
ore a game they had to carefully work out what laws they were playing by.<b=
r><br>There was an attempt to draw up an amalgamated set of laws at Cambrid=
ge University in 1848, then the Reverend JC Thring, a master at Uppingham S=
chool, had another go in 1862. But it was when representatives of Barnes, B=
lackheath, Blackheath Proprietary School, Charterhouse School (who ultimate=
ly decided not to join), Civil Service, Crusaders, Crystal Palace, Forest o=
f Leytonstone, Kensington School, No Names (Kilburn), Perceval House (Black=
heath) and Surbiton met in Covent Garden that modern football truly began.<=
